// Class 12 Chemistry Unit X: Biomolecules (Official Syllabus - 07 Marks)
// Comprehensive Deep Reference Book Standard (O.P. Tandon / Pradeep's Standard)

export const c12Chem10HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">Quick Glossary &amp; Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Essential Core Concepts &amp; Key Definitions &bull; Unit X: Biomolecules (Official Syllabus - 07 Marks)</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">1. Carbohydrates &amp; Reducing Sugars:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Optically active polyhydroxy aldehydes or polyhydroxy ketones, or substances that yield such compounds upon hydrolysis. <b>Reducing Sugars</b> contain free or potential hemiacetal/hemiketal aldehydic/ketonic groups that reduce Tollens&apos; and Fehling&apos;s reagents (all monosaccharides, maltose, lactose; sucrose is non-reducing).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">2. Anomers &amp; Mutarotation:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Anomers</b> are cyclic diastereomers of sugars differing in spatial configuration solely at the hemiacetal/hemiketal carbon (C-1 in aldoses, C-2 in ketoses), called the <b>anomeric carbon</b> (&alpha; and &beta; forms). <b>Mutarotation</b> is the spontaneous change in specific optical rotation of an optically active sugar in aqueous solution until equilibrium is reached (+112&deg; for &alpha;-D-glucose &rarr; +52.7&deg; &larr; +19&deg; for &beta;-D-glucose).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">3. Glycosidic &amp; Peptide Linkages:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Glycosidic Linkage:</b> The ether oxide linkage (-C-O-C-) joining two monosaccharide units via elimination of water. <b>Peptide Linkage (-CO-NH-):</b> An amide covalent bond formed between the &alpha;-carboxyl group of one amino acid and the &alpha;-amino group of another amino acid with elimination of water.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">4. Amino Acids, Zwitterion &amp; Isoelectric Point (pI):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Compounds containing both an amino (-NH<sub>2</sub>) and a carboxyl (-COOH) group. In aqueous solution, the -COOH transfers a proton to -NH<sub>2</sub> forming an internal dipolar <b>Zwitterion (H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-CHR-COO<sup>-</sup>)</b>. The specific pH at which an amino acid carries no net electrical charge and does not migrate in an electric field is its <b>Isoelectric Point (pI)</b>.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">5. Protein Structures &amp; Denaturation:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Primary (1&deg;):</b> Specific linear sequence of amino acids; <b>Secondary (2&deg;):</b> &alpha;-Helix and &beta;-Pleated Sheet stabilized by H-bonds; <b>Tertiary (3&deg;):</b> Overall 3D folding (Fibrous vs Globular) stabilized by disulfide bonds, H-bonds, salt bridges, van der Waals; <b>Quaternary (4&deg;):</b> Assembly of multiple subunits (e.g. Haemoglobin). <b>Denaturation:</b> Physical or chemical disruption (heat, pH) that unfolds 2&deg;, 3&deg;, 4&deg; structures with loss of biological activity, leaving 1&deg; structure intact.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">6. Nucleic Acids (DNA &amp; RNA):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Polynucleotides joined by 3&apos;-5&apos; phosphodiester linkages. A <b>Nucleoside</b> = Sugar + Nitrogenous Base; a <b>Nucleotide</b> = Nucleoside + Phosphate group. <b>DNA:</b> 2-Deoxy-D-ribose sugar, bases A, T, G, C, double-stranded antiparallel helix with complementary base pairing (A=T, G&equiv;C). <b>RNA:</b> D-Ribose sugar, bases A, U, G, C, single-stranded.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">7. Vitamins (Fat-Soluble vs Water-Soluble):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Essential micronutrients required in small amounts. <b>Fat-Soluble:</b> Vitamins A, D, E, K (stored in liver and adipose tissue). <b>Water-Soluble:</b> Vitamins B-complex and C (cannot be stored in the body except B12, readily excreted in urine).</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 1 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00B0FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">1. Classification of Carbohydrates &amp; Reducing Sugars</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Classification based on Behaviour on Hydrolysis</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Monosaccharides:</b> Cannot be hydrolysed further into simpler polyhydroxy aldehydes/ketones (e.g. Glucose C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub>, Fructose C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub>, Ribose C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O<sub>5</sub>).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Oligosaccharides:</b> Yield 2 to 10 monosaccharide units on hydrolysis:<br>\n        &bull; <b>Disaccharides (yield 2 units):</b> Sucrose (Glucose + Fructose), Maltose (Glucose + Glucose), Lactose (Glucose + Galactose).<br>\n        &bull; <b>Trisaccharides (yield 3 units):</b> Raffinose.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Polysaccharides (Non-Sugars):</b> High-molecular-weight polymers yielding a large number of monosaccharide units on hydrolysis (e.g. Starch, Cellulose, Glycogen).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Reducing vs Non-Reducing Sugars</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Reducing Sugars:</b> Contain free or potential aldehydic (-CHO) or &alpha;-hydroxy ketonic (-CO-CH<sub>2</sub>OH) groups and reduce Tollens&apos; reagent (to silver mirror) and Fehling&apos;s solution (to red Cu<sub>2</sub>O ppt). Examples: All monosaccharides (Glucose, Fructose, Galactose) and disaccharides like <b>Maltose and Lactose</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Non-Reducing Sugars:</b> The reducing groups (hemiacetal/hemiketal OH) of both monosaccharide units are involved in glycosidic bond formation (no free anomeric -OH). Example: <b>Sucrose</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00B0FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">2. D-Glucose: Structure, Preparation &amp; 6 Chemical Proofs</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #00B0FF; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">6 EXPERIMENTAL CHEMICAL EVIDENCES FOR OPEN-CHAIN STRUCTURE OF GLUCOSE</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">1. Six Carbon Unbranched Straight Chain:</b> Prolonged heating with <b>HI and Red Phosphorus</b> at 373 K yields <b>n-Hexane</b> (CH<sub>3</sub>-(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>4</sub>-CH<sub>3</sub>), proving all 6 carbons are linked in a straight chain.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">2. Presence of Carbonyl Group (&gt;C=O):</b> Reacts with <b>Hydroxylamine (NH<sub>2</sub>OH)</b> to form an <b>Oxime</b>, and adds <b>HCN</b> to form a <b>Cyanohydrin</b>.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">3. Carbonyl Group is an Aldehyde (-CHO):</b> Mild oxidation with <b>Bromine Water (Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O)</b> oxidizes glucose to a 6-carbon monocarboxylic acid, <b>Gluconic acid [HOCH<sub>2</sub>-(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>-COOH]</b>.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">4. Presence of Five Hydroxyl Groups (-OH):</b> Acetylation with <b>Acetic Anhydride</b> in the presence of ZnCl<sub>2</sub> yields <b>Glucose Pentaacetate</b>, confirming 5 -OH groups attached to 5 different carbons.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">5. Presence of One Primary Alcohol Group (-CH<sub>2</sub>OH):</b> Oxidation with concentrated <b>Nitric Acid (HNO<sub>3</sub>)</b> oxidizes both the terminal -CHO and -CH<sub>2</sub>OH groups to give a 6-carbon dicarboxylic acid, <b>Saccharic acid / Glucaric acid [HOOC-(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>-COOH]</b>.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">6. D-(+) Configuration:</b> The -OH group on the lowest asymmetric carbon (C-5) lies on the right-hand side in the Fischer projection (resembling D-(+)-glyceraldehyde).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00B0FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">3. Cyclic Structure of Glucose, Anomers &amp; Mutarotation</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Limitations of Open-Chain Structure of Glucose</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Despite having an aldehyde group: (1) Glucose does not restore pink colour with Schiff&apos;s reagent; (2) Does not form bisulphite addition product with NaHSO<sub>3</sub>; (3) Glucose pentaacetate does not react with hydroxylamine (proves absence of free -CHO group); (4) Exists in two distinct crystalline forms (&alpha; and &beta;) with different melting points and optical rotations.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Haworth Pyranose Structures &amp; Anomers (&alpha; vs &beta;)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Glucose forms a six-membered cyclic hemiacetal ring between the C-1 carbonyl and C-5 hydroxyl group, called <b>Glucopyranose</b>. This generates a new chiral carbon at C-1 (anomeric carbon):<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; &alpha;-D-(+)-Glucopyranose:</b> Anomeric -OH group at C-1 is directed <b>downwards</b> (trans to -CH<sub>2</sub>OH at C-5). MP = 419 K, specific rotation [&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = <b>+112&deg;</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; &beta;-D-(+)-Glucopyranose:</b> Anomeric -OH group at C-1 is directed <b>upwards</b> (cis to -CH<sub>2</sub>OH at C-5). MP = 423 K, specific rotation [&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = <b>+19&deg;</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Mutarotation Equilibrium:</b> In aqueous solution, both forms interconvert through the open-chain form until an equilibrium specific rotation of <b>+52.7&deg;</b> (36% &alpha; + 64% &beta;) is attained.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00B0FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">4. Disaccharides: Sucrose, Maltose &amp; Lactose (Invert Sugar)</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Sucrose (Cane Sugar) &amp; Invert Sugar Phenomenon</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Structure:</b> Composed of <b>&alpha;-D-Glucopyranose and &beta;-D-Fructofuranose</b> linked by an <b>&alpha;-1,&beta;-2 glycosidic bond</b> between C-1 of glucose and C-2 of fructose. Since both anomeric carbons are locked, sucrose is a <b>Non-Reducing Sugar</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Inversion of Cane Sugar:</b> Sucrose is dextrorotatory ([&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = <b>+66.5&deg;</b>). Upon hydrolysis by dilute acid or enzyme <i>invertase</i>, it yields an equimolar mixture of D-(+)-glucose ([&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = +52.7&deg;) and D-(-)-fructose ([&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = -92.4&deg;). Because the laevorotation of fructose exceeds the dextrorotation of glucose, the resulting hydrolysate is <b>laevorotatory ([&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = -39.7&deg;)</b>. This optical sign reversal is called <b>Inversion of Sugar</b>, and the mixture is called <b>Invert Sugar</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Maltose &amp; Lactose (Reducing Disaccharides)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Maltose (Malt Sugar):</b> Composed of two <b>&alpha;-D-Glucose</b> units linked by an <b>&alpha;-1,4-glycosidic bond</b>. The hemiacetal group at C-1 of the second glucose unit is free &rarr; <b>Reducing Sugar</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Lactose (Milk Sugar):</b> Composed of <b>&beta;-D-Galactose and &beta;-D-Glucose</b> joined by a <b>&beta;-1,4-glycosidic bond</b>. C-1 of glucose unit is free &rarr; <b>Reducing Sugar</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 5 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00B0FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">5. Polysaccharides: Starch, Cellulose &amp; Glycogen</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #00B0FF; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">COMPARISON: Starch (Amylose vs Amylopectin), Cellulose &amp; Glycogen</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">1. Starch (Plants Storage Polysaccharide, (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O<sub>5</sub>)<sub>n</sub>):</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Amylose (15-20%):</b> Water-soluble linear unbranched polymer of &alpha;-D-glucose linked exclusively by <b>&alpha;-1,4-glycosidic bonds</b> (200-1000 units). Gives <b>deep blue colour with iodine</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Amylopectin (80-85%):</b> Water-insoluble branched polymer. Linear chains contain <b>&alpha;-1,4-glycosidic bonds</b>, while branching points occur every 20-25 glucose units via <b>&alpha;-1,6-glycosidic bonds</b>.<br><br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">2. Cellulose (Plant Structural Material):</b> Linear unbranched polymer of <b>&beta;-D-Glucose</b> linked by <b>&beta;-1,4-glycosidic bonds</b>. Hydrogen bonding between adjacent parallel chains forms rigid, tensile microfibrils. Cannot be digested by humans due to lack of enzyme <i>cellulase</i>.<br><br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">3. Glycogen (Animal Starch):</b> Primary carbohydrate storage in animal liver and muscles. Structurally similar to amylopectin but <b>much more highly branched</b> (branch points every 8-12 glucose units via &alpha;-1,6 bonds).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 6 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00B0FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">6. Amino Acids: Classification, Zwitterion &amp; Isoelectric Point</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Essential vs Non-Essential Amino Acids</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Essential Amino Acids (10):</b> Cannot be synthesized by the human body and must be supplied in diet (Valine, Leucine, Isoleucine, Phenylalanine, Methionine, Tryptophan, Threonine, Lysine, Histidine, Arginine).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Non-Essential Amino Acids (10):</b> Can be synthesized in the human body from metabolic intermediates (Glycine, Alanine, Serine, Cysteine, Aspartic acid, Glutamic acid, Asparagine, Glutamine, Tyrosine, Proline).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Optical Activity:</b> All naturally occurring &alpha;-amino acids except <b>Glycine (H<sub>2</sub>N-CH<sub>2</sub>-COOH)</b> are optically active and possess the <b>L-configuration</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Zwitterion Structure &amp; Amphoteric Behaviour</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        In aqueous solution, the carboxyl group loses a proton and the amino group accepts a proton, forming an internal dipolar ion (<b>Zwitterion: H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-CHR-COO<sup>-</sup></b>).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Amphoteric Nature:</b> In acidic medium (low pH), zwitterion accepts a proton to form a cation (H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-CHR-COOH) migrating to cathode. In alkaline medium (high pH), it loses a proton to form an anion (H<sub>2</sub>N-CHR-COO<sup>-</sup>) migrating to anode.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Isoelectric Point (pI):</b> The intermediate pH at which the dipolar zwitterion has net zero charge and does not migrate towards either electrode in electrophoresis.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 7 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00B0FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">7. Proteins: 4 Structural Levels &amp; Denaturation</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #00B0FF; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">FOUR LEVELS OF PROTEIN ARCHITECTURE</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">1. Primary Structure (1&deg;):</b> The exact linear sequence in which amino acids are joined by covalent peptide bonds (-CO-NH-). Any change in sequence disrupts biological activity (e.g. Sickle-cell anaemia where Glu is replaced by Val at position 6 of &beta;-chain).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">2. Secondary Structure (2&deg;):</b> Conformation of polypeptide backbone stabilized by <b>Hydrogen Bonds</b> between &gt;C=O and -NH- groups:<br>\n      &bull; <b>&alpha;-Helix:</b> Right-handed coil stabilized by <b>intramolecular H-bonds</b> between C=O of amino acid n and N-H of amino acid (n+4) (e.g. &alpha;-Keratin in hair, wool).<br>\n      &bull; <b>&beta;-Pleated Sheet:</b> Polypeptide chains lie side by side in parallel or antiparallel sheets held by <b>intermolecular H-bonds</b> (e.g. Silk fibroin).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">3. Tertiary Structure (3&deg;):</b> Overall three-dimensional folding of polypeptide chains giving <b>Fibrous</b> (insoluble, structural: collagen, keratin) or <b>Globular</b> (soluble, functional: enzymes, myoglobin, insulin) shapes, stabilized by <b>disulfide bonds (-S-S-), ionic bonds/salt bridges, hydrophobic interactions, and H-bonds</b>.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">4. Quaternary Structure (4&deg;):</b> Spatial arrangement and assembly of two or more independent polypeptide subunits (e.g. <b>Haemoglobin</b> consisting of 4 subunits: 2&alpha; + 2&beta; chains).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Denaturation of Proteins</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      When a native protein is subjected to physical change (heat) or chemical change (pH change, salts), hydrogen bonds and tertiary interactions are disrupted; globules unfold, helices uncoil, and the protein precipitates with complete loss of biological activity.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">CRUCIAL BOARD POINT:</b> During denaturation, <b>Secondary (2&deg;), Tertiary (3&deg;), and Quaternary (4&deg;) structures are completely destroyed, but the Primary (1&deg;) covalent peptide structure remains 100% intact</b>.<br>\n      <i>Examples: Coagulation of egg white (albumin) upon boiling; Curdling of milk (lactic acid bacteria lower pH causing precipitation of casein).</i>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 8 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00B0FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">8. Enzymes, Lipids &amp; Hormones</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Enzymes (Biocatalysts)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Proteins that catalyze biochemical reactions with immense efficiency (rate enhancement of 10<sup>8</sup> &mdash; 10<sup>20</sup> times) and strict substrate specificity by lowering activation energy via the lock-and-key / induced-fit mechanism. Optimal at body temperature (310 K) and physiological pH (7.4).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Hormones (Chemical Messengers)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Molecules synthesized by endocrine ductless glands that regulate metabolic homeostasis:<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Steroid Hormones:</b> Estrogens, Progesterone, Testosterone, Cortisone (contain cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene ring).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Peptide / Protein Hormones:</b> <b>Insulin</b> (51 amino acids, regulates blood glucose), Glucagon, Oxytocin.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Amine Derivatives:</b> Epinephrine (adrenaline, fight-or-flight response), Thyroxine (iodinated tyrosine, regulates basal metabolism).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 9 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00B0FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">9. Nucleic Acids: Chemical Composition, DNA vs RNA &amp; Double Helix</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #00B0FF; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">CHEMICAL ANATOMY OF NUCLEIC ACIDS</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">1. Three Fundamental Components:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Pentose Sugar:</b> &beta;-D-2-deoxyribose (in DNA) vs &beta;-D-ribose (in RNA, has -OH at C-2&apos;).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Nitrogenous Bases:</b> <b>Purines:</b> Adenine (A), Guanine (G). <b>Pyrimidines:</b> Cytosine (C), Thymine (T, DNA only), Uracil (U, RNA only).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Phosphoric Acid:</b> Links nucleotides via <b>3&apos;-5&apos; phosphodiester bonds</b>.<br><br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">2. Nucleoside vs Nucleotide:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Nucleoside = Base + Sugar</b> (linked via &beta;-glycosidic bond at C-1&apos; of sugar).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Nucleotide = Nucleoside + Phosphate</b> (esterified at C-5&apos; of sugar).<br><br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">3. Watson-Crick Double Helix Model of DNA:</b><br>\n      &bull; Two antiparallel polynucleotide strands (5&apos;&rarr;3&apos; and 3&apos;&rarr;5&apos;) wound around a common central axis.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Complementary Base Pairing:</b> Adenine pairs exclusively with Thymine via <b>2 Hydrogen Bonds (A=T)</b>; Guanine pairs exclusively with Cytosine via <b>3 Hydrogen Bonds (G&equiv;C)</b>.<br>\n      &bull; Pitch of helix = 3.4 nm containing 10 base pairs per turn (distance between adjacent base pairs = 0.34 nm).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 10 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00B0FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">10. Vitamins: Classification, Sources &amp; Deficiency Diseases</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #00B0FF; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">COMPLETE BOARD VITAMIN MATRIX</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.7; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">FAT-SOLUBLE VITAMINS:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Vitamin A (Retinol):</b> Carrots, butter, fish liver oil &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> <b>Night Blindness, Xerophthalmia (keratinisation of cornea)</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Vitamin D (Calciferol):</b> Sunlight, egg yolk, fish liver oil &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> <b>Rickets (in children), Osteomalacia (soft bones in adults)</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Vitamin E (Tocopherol):</b> Wheat germ oil, sunflower oil &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> <b>Muscular weakness, increased fragility of RBCs, Sterility</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Vitamin K (Phylloquinone):</b> Green leafy vegetables &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> <b>Increased blood clotting time, Haemorrhagic disease</b>.<br><br>\n\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">WATER-SOLUBLE VITAMINS:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Vitamin B<sub>1</sub> (Thiamine):</b> Yeast, whole grains &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> <b>Beriberi (loss of appetite, muscular weakness)</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Vitamin B<sub>2</sub> (Riboflavin):</b> Milk, egg white, liver &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> <b>Cheilosis (fissuring at corners of mouth), glossitis</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Vitamin B<sub>3</sub> (Niacin / Nicotinic acid):</b> Meat, fish &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> <b>Pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia)</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Vitamin B<sub>6</sub> (Pyridoxine):</b> Cereals, gram &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> <b>Convulsions</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Vitamin B<sub>12</sub> (Cyanocobalamin - contains Cobalt):</b> Meat, fish, egg (not present in plants!) &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> <b>Pernicious Anaemia (RBC deficiency in haemoglobin)</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Vitamin C (Ascorbic acid):</b> Citrus fruits, amla, tomato &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> <b>Scurvy (bleeding gums, delayed wound healing)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 11 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00B0FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">11. Master Revision Formula &amp; Biomolecules Summary Cheat Sheet</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">1. Master Biomolecule Linkages &amp; Units:</b>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Sucrose:</b> &alpha;-D-Glucopyranose + &beta;-D-Fructofuranose (&alpha;-1,&beta;-2 bond) &bull; Non-reducing &bull; Invert sugar ([&alpha;]<sub>D</sub>: +66.5&deg; &rarr; -39.7&deg;).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Maltose:</b> &alpha;-D-Glucose + &alpha;-D-Glucose (&alpha;-1,4 bond) &bull; Reducing sugar.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Lactose:</b> &beta;-D-Galactose + &beta;-D-Glucose (&beta;-1,4 bond) &bull; Reducing sugar.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Starch:</b> Amylose (&alpha;-1,4 linear) + Amylopectin (&alpha;-1,4 linear and &alpha;-1,6 branched).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Cellulose:</b> &beta;-D-Glucose (&beta;-1,4 linear).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Protein:</b> Amino acids linked by peptide bonds (-CO-NH-).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Nucleic Acids:</b> Nucleotides linked by 3&apos;-5&apos; phosphodiester bonds.<br><br>\n\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">2. Diagnostic Distinction Matrix:</b>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; DNA vs RNA:</b> 2-Deoxyribose (DNA) vs Ribose (RNA); Thymine (DNA) vs Uracil (RNA); Double helix (DNA) vs Single strand (RNA).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Base Pairing:</b> A=T (2 H-bonds) and G&equiv;C (3 H-bonds).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #00B0FF;\">&bull; Denaturation:</b> Destroys 2&deg;, 3&deg;, 4&deg; protein structures; 1&deg; primary sequence remains intact.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

export const c12Chem10HtmlSolutions = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #00B0FF; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 21px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Class 12 Chemistry Unit X: Biomolecules (07 Marks)\n    </h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Master 100 Board Standard Q&amp;A (Section A: 1-40 VSA &bull; Section B: 41-80 SA &bull; Section C: 81-100 LA Derivations)\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.12); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 30px 0 20px 0; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">Section A: Very Short Answer (VSA) Questions (1 Mark each)</h2>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q1: Define Carbohydrates according to modern chemical definition.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Optically active <b>polyhydroxy aldehydes or polyhydroxy ketones</b>, or substances that produce such compounds upon hydrolysis.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q2: What is the molecular formula of Glucose and Fructose? How do their functional groups differ?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Both share the molecular formula <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub></b>. Glucose is an <b>Aldohexose</b> (contains an aldehyde group -CHO at C-1), while Fructose is a <b>Ketohexose</b> (contains a keto group &gt;C=O at C-2).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q3: What product is obtained when D-Glucose is heated with concentrated HI and Red Phosphorus for a prolonged time?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>n-Hexane (CH<sub>3</sub>-(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>4</sub>-CH<sub>3</sub>)</b>, proving that all six carbon atoms in glucose are linked in a straight, unbranched chain.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q4: What is obtained when D-Glucose is treated with mild oxidising agent Bromine water (Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Gluconic acid [HOCH<sub>2</sub>-(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>-COOH]</b>, confirming that the carbonyl group in glucose is an aldehyde (-CHO).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q5: What is formed upon oxidation of D-Glucose with concentrated Nitric acid (HNO<sub>3</sub>)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Saccharic acid / Glucaric acid [HOOC-(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>-COOH]</b>, confirming the presence of one primary alcohol (-CH<sub>2</sub>OH) group at the terminal position.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q6: What is formed when D-Glucose is treated with Acetic anhydride in the presence of ZnCl<sub>2</sub>?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Glucose Pentaacetate</b>, confirming the presence of five hydroxyl (-OH) groups attached to five different carbon atoms.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q7: What are Anomers? Give an example.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Diastereomeric cyclic forms of a sugar that differ in spatial configuration solely at the <b>anomeric carbon (C-1 in aldoses, C-2 in ketoses)</b>. Example: <b>&alpha;-D-Glucopyranose and &beta;-D-Glucopyranose</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q8: What is Mutarotation? State the equilibrium specific rotation of D-Glucose in water.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">The spontaneous change in specific optical rotation of an optically active carbohydrate solution over time until equilibrium is established. For D-glucose, [&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> shifts from +112&deg; (&alpha;) or +19&deg; (&beta;) to an equilibrium value of <b>+52.7&deg;</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q9: What is a Glycosidic Linkage?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">An <b>oxide / ether bond (-C-O-C-)</b> formed between the anomeric carbon of one monosaccharide unit and a hydroxyl oxygen of another monosaccharide unit with the elimination of a water molecule.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q10: Name the monosaccharide units present in: (i) Sucrose, (ii) Maltose, (iii) Lactose.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Sucrose:</b> &alpha;-D-Glucose + &beta;-D-Fructose.<br><b>(ii) Maltose:</b> &alpha;-D-Glucose + &alpha;-D-Glucose.<br><b>(iii) Lactose:</b> &beta;-D-Galactose + &beta;-D-Glucose.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q11: Why is Sucrose a Non-Reducing Sugar while Maltose is a Reducing Sugar?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">In <b>Sucrose</b>, the glycosidic bond is formed between the anomeric carbons of both units (C-1 of glucose and C-2 of fructose), leaving no free reducing hemiacetal group. In <b>Maltose</b>, C-1 of the second glucose unit has a free hemiacetal -OH group.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q12: What is Invert Sugar? Why is it called so?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">An equimolar mixture of D-(+)-glucose and D-(-)-fructose obtained by hydrolysis of sucrose. It is called invert sugar because the sign of specific optical rotation <b>inverts from dextrorotatory (+66.5&deg;) to laevorotatory (-39.7&deg;)</b> upon hydrolysis.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q13: Name the two structural components of Starch. How do they differ in solubility and branching?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Amylose (15-20%):</b> Water-soluble, linear unbranched chain of &alpha;-D-glucose linked by &alpha;-1,4-glycosidic bonds.<br><b>2. Amylopectin (80-85%):</b> Water-insoluble, branched polymer with &alpha;-1,4-linear chains and &alpha;-1,6-glycosidic branching points.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q14: What is the structural monomer and glycosidic linkage in Cellulose?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Polymer of <b>&beta;-D-Glucose</b> linked exclusively by <b>&beta;-1,4-glycosidic bonds</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q15: Why can humans not digest Cellulose as a source of energy?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because the human digestive tract lacks the specific enzyme <b>cellulase</b> required to hydrolyse the &beta;-1,4-glycosidic bonds of cellulose.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q16: What is Glycogen? Where is it stored in the animal body?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">The storage polysaccharide of animals (also called <b>animal starch</b>), structurally similar to amylopectin but much more highly branched. Stored primarily in the <b>liver and skeletal muscles</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q17: What are Essential Amino Acids? Name any two.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Amino acids that cannot be synthesized by the human body and must be supplied through dietary intake. Examples: <b>Valine, Leucine, Lysine, Methionine</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q18: Which &alpha;-amino acid is optically INACTIVE and why?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Glycine (H<sub>2</sub>N-CH<sub>2</sub>-COOH)</b>, because its &alpha;-carbon atom is bonded to two identical hydrogen atoms and is therefore achiral.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q19: What is a Zwitterion in amino acids?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">An internal dipolar ion formed when the carboxyl group (-COOH) donates a proton to the amino group (-NH<sub>2</sub>) within the same amino acid molecule: <b>H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-CHR-COO<sup>-</sup></b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q20: What is the Isoelectric Point (pI) of an amino acid?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">The specific pH at which an amino acid exists predominantly as a neutral dipolar zwitterion with net zero electrical charge and does not migrate towards either electrode in an electric field.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q21: What is a Peptide Linkage? Write its general chemical structure.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">An amide bond formed between the &alpha;-carboxyl group of one amino acid and the &alpha;-amino group of another amino acid: <b>-CO-NH-</b> with elimination of H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q22: Differentiate between Fibrous Proteins and Globular Proteins with one example of each.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Fibrous Proteins:</b> Polypeptide chains run parallel in linear fiber-like sheets held by H-bonds/disulfide bonds; insoluble in water (e.g. <b>Keratin</b> in hair, <b>Myosin</b> in muscles).<br><b>Globular Proteins:</b> Polypeptide chains fold into spherical compact shapes; soluble in water (e.g. <b>Insulin, Albumin</b>).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q23: What bonds stabilize the Secondary structure (&alpha;-Helix and &beta;-Pleated Sheet) of proteins?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Intermolecular and Intramolecular Hydrogen Bonds</b> between the &gt;C=O group of one peptide bond and the -NH- group of another peptide bond.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q24: What is Denaturation of Proteins? What happens to the primary structure during denaturation?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">The disruption of native secondary, tertiary, and quaternary conformations of a protein by heat, pH changes, or chemicals, leading to loss of biological activity. The <b>Primary structure (1&deg; sequence of amino acids) remains completely intact</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q25: Give two everyday examples of protein denaturation.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">1. <b>Coagulation of egg white (albumin)</b> upon boiling.<br>2. <b>Curdling of milk</b> by lactic acid bacteria (precipitation of casein).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q26: What are Enzymes? State their chemical nature.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Biological catalysts produced by living cells that accelerate biochemical reactions with extreme specificity and efficiency. All enzymes are <b>globular proteins</b> (with the exception of ribozymes).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q27: Name the pentose sugar present in: (i) DNA, (ii) RNA.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) In DNA:</b> <b>&beta;-D-2-Deoxyribose</b>.<br><b>(ii) In RNA:</b> <b>&beta;-D-Ribose</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q28: Name the four nitrogenous bases present in DNA.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Adenine (A), Guanine (G), Cytosine (C), and Thymine (T)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q29: Name the nitrogenous base present in RNA in place of Thymine.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Uracil (U)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q30: What is the structural difference between a Nucleoside and a Nucleotide?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">A <b>Nucleoside</b> consists of a nitrogenous base attached to a pentose sugar (Base + Sugar). A <b>Nucleotide</b> consists of a nucleoside esterified with a phosphoric acid group at the C-5&apos; position (Base + Sugar + Phosphate).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q31: Name the linkage that joins successive nucleotides in a nucleic acid chain.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>3&apos;-5&apos; Phosphodiester Linkage</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q32: State the Complementary Base Pairing rule in the Watson-Crick DNA double helix.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Adenine pairs strictly with Thymine via 2 Hydrogen Bonds (A=T)</b>; <b>Guanine pairs strictly with Cytosine via 3 Hydrogen Bonds (G&equiv;C)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q33: Name the three major types of RNA present in living cells.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Messenger RNA (mRNA)</b>, <b>2. Transfer RNA (tRNA)</b>, <b>3. Ribosomal RNA (rRNA)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q34: What is DNA Fingerprinting and state one forensic application.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">A technique used to identify individuals based on unique nucleotide base sequence patterns in their DNA. Applied in <b>forensic identification of criminals, paternity testing, and resolving immigration disputes</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q35: Classify Vitamins into Fat-Soluble and Water-Soluble vitamins.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Fat-Soluble:</b> <b>Vitamins A, D, E, and K</b>.<br><b>Water-Soluble:</b> <b>Vitamin B-complex and Vitamin C</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q36: Why must Vitamin C be supplied regularly in our daily diet?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is <b>water-soluble</b> and cannot be stored in the human body; any excess is readily excreted in urine.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q37: Which Vitamin contains the trace metal Cobalt (Co)? Name its deficiency disease.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Vitamin B<sub>12</sub> (Cyanocobalamin)</b>; deficiency causes <b>Pernicious Anaemia</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q38: Name the deficiency disease caused by lack of: (i) Vitamin A, (ii) Vitamin C, (iii) Vitamin D, (iv) Vitamin K.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Vitamin A:</b> Night Blindness &amp; Xerophthalmia.<br><b>(ii) Vitamin C:</b> Scurvy.<br><b>(iii) Vitamin D:</b> Rickets (in children) / Osteomalacia (in adults).<br><b>(iv) Vitamin K:</b> Increased blood clotting time / Haemorrhage.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q39: Name the hormone that regulates blood glucose levels in the human body. State its chemical nature.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Insulin</b>, a <b>peptide hormone</b> containing 51 amino acids arranged in two polypeptide chains (A and B) linked by disulfide bonds.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q40: What is the primary biological function of Lipids in living organisms?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Long-term <b>energy storage</b>, structural components of cellular membranes (phospholipids), thermal insulation, and precursors of steroid hormones.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.12); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 30px 0 20px 0; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">Section B: Short Answer (SA) Questions (2 &amp; 3 Marks each)</h2>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q41: Explain with chemical equations why D-Glucose is classified as an Aldohexose and D-Fructose as a Ketohexose.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Glucose (Aldohexose):</b> Contains 6 carbons and an aldehyde (-CHO) group. Oxidized by mild bromine water to <b>Gluconic acid</b>: HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CHO + [O] &rarr; (Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O) &rarr; HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>COOH.<br><b>Fructose (Ketohexose):</b> Contains 6 carbons and a ketone (&gt;C=O) group at C-2. Fructose resists bromine water oxidation (does not form gluconic acid), confirming the presence of a ketonic group at C-2.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q42: Give chemical reactions of D-Glucose with: (i) Hydroxylamine, (ii) Hydrogen Cyanide (HCN), (iii) Concentrated Nitric acid.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) With NH<sub>2</sub>OH:</b> Forms Glucose Oxime: HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CHO + NH<sub>2</sub>OH &rarr; <b>HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CH=N-OH</b> + H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><b>(ii) With HCN:</b> Forms Glucose Cyanohydrin: HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CHO + HCN &rarr; <b>HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CH(OH)CN</b>.<br><b>(iii) With conc. HNO<sub>3</sub>:</b> Oxidizes terminal -CHO and -CH<sub>2</sub>OH groups to <b>Saccharic acid [HOOC-(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>-COOH]</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q43: State four experimental observations that proved the Open-Chain structure of Glucose was inadequate and necessitated a Cyclic structure.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">1. <b>Failure with Schiff's Reagent &amp; NaHSO<sub>3</sub>:</b> Glucose does not restore colour of Schiff's reagent nor form bisulphite adduct with NaHSO<sub>3</sub>.<br>2. <b>Glucose Pentaacetate with NH<sub>2</sub>OH:</b> The pentaacetate of glucose does not react with hydroxylamine, indicating the absence of a free -CHO group.<br>3. <b>Existence of Two Anomeric Forms:</b> Glucose crystallizes in two distinct forms, &alpha; (MP 419 K, [&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = +112&deg;) and &beta; (MP 423 K, [&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = +19&deg;).<br>4. <b>Mutarotation:</b> The specific optical rotation of freshly prepared solutions of &alpha; and &beta; forms spontaneously changes over time until reaching equilibrium (+52.7&deg;).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q44: Draw the Haworth projection structures of &alpha;-D-Glucopyranose and &beta;-D-Glucopyranose. Highlight the anomeric carbon.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">In Haworth projections for D-glucopyranose (6-membered ring containing 5 carbons and 1 ring oxygen):<br>&bull; <b>Anomeric Carbon is C-1</b>.<br>&bull; <b>&alpha;-D-Glucopyranose:</b> The -OH group at C-1 is oriented <b>downward</b> (trans to the -CH<sub>2</sub>OH group at C-5).<br>&bull; <b>&beta;-D-Glucopyranose:</b> The -OH group at C-1 is oriented <b>upward</b> (cis to the -CH<sub>2</sub>OH group at C-5).<br>&bull; Groups on the right in Fischer projection point downward in Haworth, while groups on the left point upward.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q45: Explain the phenomenon of Inversion of Cane Sugar (Sucrose) with optical rotations and chemical equation.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Chemical Equation:</b> C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub> (Sucrose) + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup> or Invertase) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> (D-(+)-Glucose) + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> (D-(-)-Fructose).<br><b>Optical Sign Inversion:</b><br>&bull; Sucrose is <b>dextrorotatory</b> with specific optical rotation [&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = <b>+66.5&deg;</b>.<br>&bull; Upon complete hydrolysis, it produces equimolar D-(+)-glucose ([&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = <b>+52.7&deg;</b>) and D-(-)-fructose ([&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = <b>-92.4&deg;</b>).<br>&bull; Because the laevorotation of fructose is much greater in magnitude than the dextrorotation of glucose, the net resultant mixture has a specific optical rotation of <b>-39.7&deg; (Laevorotatory)</b>. Hence, the process is called inversion of sugar.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q46: Compare Amylose and Amylopectin on the basis of: (i) Solubility in water, (ii) Structure and chain length, (iii) Glycosidic linkages, (iv) Reaction with Iodine.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">1. <b>Solubility:</b> Amylose is water-soluble (15-20% of starch); Amylopectin is water-insoluble (80-85%).<br>2. <b>Structure:</b> Amylose is a linear unbranched polymer of 200-1000 &alpha;-D-glucose units; Amylopectin is a highly branched polymer of up to 100,000 glucose units.<br>3. <b>Linkages:</b> Amylose contains only <b>&alpha;-1,4-glycosidic bonds</b>; Amylopectin contains <b>&alpha;-1,4-bonds in linear chains and &alpha;-1,6-glycosidic bonds at branch points</b>.<br>4. <b>Iodine Test:</b> Amylose forms a deep helical blue complex with iodine; Amylopectin gives a reddish-purple colour.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q47: Why is Cellulose an insoluble structural material while Glycogen serves as energy storage?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Cellulose</b> consists of linear unbranched chains of &beta;-D-glucose joined by &beta;-1,4-bonds. These straight chains pack closely side by side, forming extensive inter-chain <b>Hydrogen Bonds</b> that create rigid, water-insoluble, high-tensile crystalline microfibrils ideal for plant cell walls.<br><b>Glycogen</b> is a highly branched &alpha;-D-glucose polymer with compact spherical shape that exposes many non-reducing ends for rapid enzymatic hydrolysis into glucose during metabolic energy demand.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q48: Differentiate between Essential and Non-Essential Amino Acids. Give two examples of each.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Essential Amino Acids:</b> 10 amino acids that cannot be synthesized by human metabolic pathways and must be obtained from diet (e.g. <b>Valine, Leucine, Lysine, Phenylalanine</b>). Deficiency causes protein malnutrition (Kwashiorkor).<br><b>Non-Essential Amino Acids:</b> 10 amino acids that can be synthesized by the human body through transamination of metabolic intermediates (e.g. <b>Glycine, Alanine, Glutamic acid, Aspartic acid</b>).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q49: Explain the Zwitterion structure of &alpha;-amino acids and their amphoteric behaviour in acidic and alkaline media.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Amino acids possess a basic -NH<sub>2</sub> group and an acidic -COOH group. In water, intramolecular proton transfer yields the dipolar <b>Zwitterion: H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-CHR-COO<sup>-</sup></b>.<br><b>Amphoteric Behaviour:</b><br>&bull; <b>In Acidic Medium (pH &lt; pI):</b> The carboxylate ion accepts a proton: H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-CHR-COO<sup>-</sup> + H<sup>+</sup> &#8652; <b>H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-CHR-COOH (Cation)</b>, which migrates towards the <b>Cathode</b> in an electric field.<br>&bull; <b>In Alkaline Medium (pH &gt; pI):</b> The ammonium group loses a proton: H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-CHR-COO<sup>-</sup> + OH<sup>-</sup> &#8652; <b>H<sub>2</sub>N-CHR-COO<sup>-</sup> (Anion)</b>, which migrates towards the <b>Anode</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q50: Explain the differences between Primary, Secondary, Tertiary, and Quaternary structures of Proteins.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">1. <b>Primary (1&deg;):</b> The linear sequence of amino acid residues linked by covalent peptide bonds (-CO-NH-).<br>2. <b>Secondary (2&deg;):</b> Local spatial folding of polypeptide chains into <b>&alpha;-Helix</b> (intramolecular H-bonds) or <b>&beta;-Pleated Sheet</b> (intermolecular H-bonds).<br>3. <b>Tertiary (3&deg;):</b> Overall 3D spatial conformation of a single polypeptide chain into Fibrous or Globular forms, stabilized by disulfide bonds, salt bridges, H-bonds, and hydrophobic interactions.<br>4. <b>Quaternary (4&deg;):</b> Arrangement and spatial association of two or more independent polypeptide subunits into a multi-subunit protein complex (e.g. Haemoglobin).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q51: Explain the structure of &alpha;-Helix and &beta;-Pleated Sheet in secondary protein architecture.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>&alpha;-Helix:</b> A right-handed helical coil where the polypeptide backbone winds tightly. Each &gt;C=O group of an amino acid residue forms an <b>intramolecular Hydrogen Bond</b> with the -NH- group of the fourth residue ahead (n+4) in the sequence (e.g. &alpha;-Keratin).<br><b>&beta;-Pleated Sheet:</b> Polypeptide chains are stretched out nearly to full extension and arranged side by side in parallel or antiparallel strands held together by <b>intermolecular Hydrogen Bonds</b> between C=O and N-H groups of adjacent chains (e.g. Silk fibroin).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q52: What happens during the Denaturation of Proteins at the molecular level? Why is denaturation usually irreversible?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">During denaturation (caused by heat, extreme pH, or heavy metal ions), thermal kinetic energy disrupts weak non-covalent bonds (Hydrogen bonds, ionic salt bridges, hydrophobic interactions) that maintain secondary, tertiary, and quaternary structures. Helices uncoil and globular folds unfold into random tangled chains that aggregate and precipitate irreversibly (e.g. boiling egg albumin). The primary covalent peptide backbone remains intact.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q53: Differentiate between DNA and RNA across: (i) Pentose sugar, (ii) Nitrogenous bases, (iii) Strandedness, (iv) Biological function.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">1. <b>Sugar:</b> DNA contains <b>&beta;-D-2-Deoxyribose</b>; RNA contains <b>&beta;-D-Ribose</b> (has -OH at C-2&apos;).<br>2. <b>Bases:</b> DNA contains Adenine, Guanine, Cytosine, <b>Thymine</b>; RNA contains Adenine, Guanine, Cytosine, <b>Uracil</b>.<br>3. <b>Strandedness:</b> DNA is a <b>Double-stranded antiparallel helix</b>; RNA is predominantly <b>Single-stranded</b>.<br>4. <b>Function:</b> DNA stores and replicates genetic hereditary information; RNA executes protein biosynthesis (transcription &amp; translation).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q54: Describe the Watson and Crick Double Helix Model of DNA with helical parameters.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">1. <b>Antiparallel Double Strands:</b> Composed of two polynucleotide chains running in opposite directions (5&apos;&rarr;3&apos; and 3&apos;&rarr;5&apos;) wound around a central axis.<br>2. <b>Sugar-Phosphate Backbone:</b> The hydrophilic sugar-phosphate backbones form the outside of the helix, while hydrophobic bases project inward.<br>3. <b>Complementary Base Pairing:</b> A pairs with T via <b>2 Hydrogen Bonds (A=T)</b>; G pairs with C via <b>3 Hydrogen Bonds (G&equiv;C)</b>.<br>4. <b>Dimensions:</b> The diameter of the helix is <b>2.0 nm</b>; the pitch is <b>3.4 nm</b> containing exactly <b>10 base pairs per helical turn</b> (distance between adjacent base pairs = <b>0.34 nm</b>).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q55: What are Nucleosides and Nucleotides? Write the three parts of Adenosine Triphosphate (ATP).\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Nucleoside:</b> A unit composed of a nitrogenous base joined to the C-1&apos; carbon of a pentose sugar via a &beta;-glycosidic bond.<br><b>Nucleotide:</b> A phosphate ester of a nucleoside, formed by esterification of phosphoric acid at the C-5&apos; -OH of the pentose sugar.<br><b>ATP Components:</b> 1. Nitrogenous base <b>Adenine</b>, 2. Pentose sugar <b>D-Ribose</b>, 3. A chain of <b>Three Phosphate groups</b> linked by high-energy phosphoanhydride bonds.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q56: Classify Vitamins on the basis of solubility and give two deficiency diseases for each class.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Fat-Soluble Vitamins (A, D, E, K):</b> Soluble in fats and organic solvents; stored in liver and adipose tissue.<br>&bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Vitamin A &rarr; <b>Night Blindness / Xerophthalmia</b>; Vitamin D &rarr; <b>Rickets</b>.<br><b>2. Water-Soluble Vitamins (B-complex and C):</b> Soluble in water; not stored in the body (except B12), excreted in urine.<br>&bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Vitamin B<sub>1</sub> &rarr; <b>Beriberi</b>; Vitamin C &rarr; <b>Scurvy</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q57: Explain why Vitamin B12 and Vitamin C have completely different storage characteristics in the human body.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Vitamin C (Ascorbic acid):</b> Highly water-soluble; excess amounts are not reabsorbed by renal tubules and are continuously excreted in urine. Hence, it cannot be stored and must be consumed daily.<br><b>Vitamin B<sub>12</sub> (Cyanocobalamin):</b> Although water-soluble, it binds to a specific glycoprotein carrier (Intrinsic Factor) and is actively stored in significant quantities (several milligrams) in the <b>liver</b>, providing a reserve that lasts for 3 to 5 years.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q58: What are Hormones? Classify them on the basis of chemical structure with examples.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Chemical messengers secreted directly into the bloodstream by ductless endocrine glands to regulate physiological processes:<br>1. <b>Steroid Hormones:</b> Lipid-soluble, derived from cholesterol (e.g. <b>Testosterone, Estrogen, Progesterone, Cortisone</b>).<br>2. <b>Peptide &amp; Protein Hormones:</b> Water-soluble amino acid polymers (e.g. <b>Insulin, Glucagon, Oxytocin</b>).<br>3. <b>Amine Derivatives:</b> Derived from single amino acids (e.g. <b>Epinephrine / Adrenaline</b> from tyrosine, <b>Thyroxine</b>).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q59: Explain the Lock-and-Key Mechanism of Enzyme Action.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Proposed by Emil Fischer. The enzyme possesses a specific three-dimensional geometric pocket on its surface called the <b>Active Site</b>, which has complementary shape, charge, and functional groups matching the substrate molecule (the key). The substrate binds to the active site forming an <b>Enzyme-Substrate Complex (ES)</b>, lowering the activation energy for transition state formation. The substrate undergoes chemical transformation into products, which detach, freeing the enzyme unchanged: <b>E + S &#8652; [ES] &rarr; [EP] &rarr; E + P</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q60: What are Lipids? Give structural classification of Lipids with examples.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Water-insoluble biomolecules soluble in non-polar organic solvents (ether, chloroform):<br>1. <b>Simple Lipids:</b> Esters of fatty acids with alcohols, primarily <b>Triglycerides (fats and oils)</b> formed from glycerol and 3 fatty acid chains.<br>2. <b>Compound / Complex Lipids:</b> Contain additional groups (e.g. <b>Phospholipids</b> in cell membranes, Glycolipids).<br>3. <b>Derived Lipids:</b> Hydrolysis products and steroid derivatives (e.g. <b>Cholesterol</b>, fatty acids).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q61: Why does D-Glucose form a cyanohydrin with HCN but Glucose pentaacetate does not react with Hydroxylamine?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Free D-glucose in aqueous solution exists in dynamic equilibrium with a small concentration (0.02%) of the open-chain form containing a free -CHO group that reacts with HCN to form cyanohydrin. In <b>Glucose pentaacetate</b>, the C-1 anomeric -OH is converted into an acetate ester (-OCOCH<sub>3</sub>), which locks the cyclic ring and prevents mutarotation or opening into a free aldehyde form; hence, it cannot react with hydroxylamine.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q62: Differentiate between a Polypeptide and a Protein on the basis of amino acid residue count and molar mass.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Polypeptide:</b> A polymer of up to <b>100 amino acid residues</b> with molecular mass less than 10,000 u.<br><b>Protein:</b> A macromolecule composed of <b>more than 100 amino acid residues</b> with molecular mass exceeding 10,000 u that folds into a distinct biologically active 3D conformation. (Exception: <b>Insulin</b> has 51 amino acids with MW 5734 u, yet is classified as a protein due to its well-defined 3D globular structure).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q63: Explain Sickle-Cell Anaemia in terms of primary protein structure alteration.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Sickle-cell anaemia is a genetic disorder caused by a single point mutation that alters the <b>Primary structure (1&deg;) of Haemoglobin</b>. In the &beta;-globin chain, the normal sixth amino acid <b>Glutamic acid (a hydrophilic polar residue)</b> is substituted by <b>Valine (a hydrophobic non-polar residue)</b>. Under low oxygen tension, this substitution causes deoxygenated haemoglobin molecules to polymerize into rigid insoluble fibers, deforming RBCs into a sickle shape.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q64: What is the biological function of: (i) Messenger RNA (mRNA), (ii) Transfer RNA (tRNA), (iii) Ribosomal RNA (rRNA)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) mRNA:</b> Carries the genetic code transcribed from DNA in the nucleus to the ribosomes in the cytoplasm.<br><b>(ii) tRNA:</b> Acts as an adaptor molecule that reads mRNA codons and transfers corresponding specific amino acids to the growing polypeptide chain during translation.<br><b>(iii) rRNA:</b> Forms the structural and catalytic core of ribosomes, catalyzing peptide bond formation.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q65: Explain the term 'Invert Sugar' and explain why it is sweeter than ordinary cane sugar.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Invert sugar is the equimolar mixture of D-(+)-glucose and D-(-)-fructose formed by acid or enzymatic hydrolysis of sucrose. It is <b>sweeter than sucrose</b> because free D-fructose is the sweetest naturally occurring sugar (relative sweetness &approx; 173 compared to sucrose at 100 and glucose at 74).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q66: Account for: (i) Glucose and Fructose give the same Osazone with excess Phenylhydrazine, (ii) Amino acids have higher melting points than corresponding halo acids.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Osazone Formation:</b> Phenylhydrazine reacts only at C-1 and C-2 of sugars. Since D-glucose and D-fructose have identical configurations at C-3, C-4, and C-5, both yield identical <b>Glucosazone (Fructosazone)</b> crystals.<br><b>(ii) Melting Points of Amino Acids:</b> Due to their dipolar <b>Zwitterion structure (H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-CHR-COO<sup>-</sup>)</b>, amino acids have strong electrostatic ionic crystal lattices, giving them high melting points (200-300 &deg;C) like inorganic salts.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q67: Explain the biochemical role of Vitamin K in blood coagulation.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Vitamin K is a necessary cofactor for the enzyme &gamma;-glutamyl carboxylase, which carboxylates specific glutamic acid residues on <b>Prothrombin (Factor II) and other clotting factors (VII, IX, X)</b> in the liver. This &gamma;-carboxylation allows clotting factors to bind calcium ions (Ca<sup>2+</sup>) and adhere to phospholipid membranes at injury sites to form fibrin blood clots.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q68: What is Pellagra? Which vitamin deficiency causes it and what are its clinical symptoms?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Pellagra is a deficiency disease caused by lack of <b>Vitamin B<sub>3</sub> (Niacin / Nicotinic acid)</b>. It is characterized by the '4 Ds': <b>Dermatitis</b> (skin rashes on sun-exposed areas), <b>Diarrhoea</b>, <b>Dementia</b> (mental confusion and memory loss), and eventually Death if untreated.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q69: What is Scurvy? Explain its biochemical origin relating to collagen synthesis.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Scurvy is caused by deficiency of <b>Vitamin C (Ascorbic acid)</b>. Ascorbic acid is the required cofactor for enzymes <i>prolyl hydroxylase</i> and <i>lysyl hydroxylase</i> that synthesize hydroxyproline and hydroxylysine essential for stabilizing the triple helix of <b>Collagen</b>. Without Vitamin C, defective collagen leads to fragile blood vessels, bleeding gums, loose teeth, and poor wound healing.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q70: How will you distinguish between Glucose and Starch using simple chemical tests?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Iodine Test:</b> Starch forms a <b>deep intense blue-black colour</b> with iodine solution; Glucose gives no colour change.<br><b>2. Fehling's Test:</b> Glucose (a reducing monosaccharide) reduces Fehling's solution giving a <b>red precipitate of Cu<sub>2</sub>O</b>; Starch (a non-reducing polysaccharide) gives no reaction.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q71: Write the Haworth projection formula for: (i) &alpha;-D-Fructofuranose, (ii) &beta;-D-Fructofuranose.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">In Haworth projections for D-fructofuranose (5-membered ring containing 4 carbons C-2 to C-5 and 1 oxygen atom):<br>&bull; <b>Anomeric Carbon is C-2</b>.<br>&bull; <b>&alpha;-D-Fructofuranose:</b> The anomeric -OH group at C-2 is oriented <b>downward</b>.<br>&bull; <b>&beta;-D-Fructofuranose:</b> The anomeric -OH group at C-2 is oriented <b>upward</b> (cis to the -CH<sub>2</sub>OH group at C-5).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q72: What is Coenzyme and Prosthetic Group? Give an example.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Coenzyme:</b> A non-protein organic molecule (often derived from water-soluble vitamins, e.g. <b>NAD<sup>+</sup></b> containing niacin, <b>FAD</b> containing riboflavin) that associates transiently with an enzyme to assist catalytic function.<br><b>Prosthetic Group:</b> A non-protein cofactor that is permanently and tightly bound to the enzyme protein by covalent bonds (e.g. <b>Haem</b> in haemoglobin and catalase).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q73: Explain the term 'Genetic Code' and its four universal characteristics.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">The sequence of nitrogenous bases in mRNA that specifies the sequence of amino acids during protein synthesis:<br>1. <b>Triplet Nature:</b> Each codon consists of 3 consecutive nucleotides (64 codons for 20 amino acids).<br>2. <b>Degeneracy:</b> Most amino acids are coded by more than one codon.<br>3. <b>Non-overlapping &amp; Comma-less:</b> Codons are read continuously without punctuation.<br>4. <b>Universal:</b> A codon codes for the same amino acid in all living organisms (e.g. AUG codes for Methionine).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q74: Explain the difference between Hydrolysis of DNA vs RNA.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Complete chemical hydrolysis of <b>DNA</b> yields &beta;-D-2-deoxyribose, phosphoric acid, and four bases (A, G, C, T). Complete hydrolysis of <b>RNA</b> yields &beta;-D-ribose, phosphoric acid, and four bases (A, G, C, U). Furthermore, RNA is readily hydrolysed by dilute alkali due to the presence of the 2&apos;-OH group, whereas DNA is stable to alkaline hydrolysis.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q75: What is Cheilosis and which vitamin deficiency causes it?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Cheilosis is an inflammatory condition characterized by painful fissures and cracks at the corners of the mouth and lips, caused by the deficiency of <b>Vitamin B<sub>2</sub> (Riboflavin)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q76: Explain the structural role of Disulfide bonds in stabilizing protein tertiary structure.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Disulfide bonds (-S-S-) are strong covalent bonds formed by oxidation of the sulfhydryl (-SH) groups of two <b>Cysteine</b> amino acid residues located at different positions along a folded polypeptide chain. These covalent cross-links lock the tertiary conformation firmly in place (e.g. in Insulin and Keratin).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q77: What is Xerophthalmia? Which vitamin deficiency causes it?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">An eye disease characterized by abnormal dryness, opacity, and keratinisation of the cornea and conjunctiva, leading to permanent blindness, caused by severe deficiency of <b>Vitamin A (Retinol)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q78: What are Anabolic and Catabolic hormones? Give one example of each.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Anabolic Hormones:</b> Stimulate biosyntheses and tissue growth (e.g. <b>Insulin</b> promotes glycogen and protein synthesis; <b>Testosterone</b> stimulates muscle hypertrophy).<br><b>Catabolic Hormones:</b> Stimulate the breakdown of complex biomolecules to release energy (e.g. <b>Glucagon</b> stimulates glycogenolysis; <b>Cortisol</b> stimulates gluconeogenesis).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q79: Explain why Amylose gives a blue colour with Iodine while Cellulose does not.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Amylose</b> forms a continuous right-handed helical coil with hydrophobic hollow channels of precise diameter that trap molecular polyiodide ions (I<sub>5</sub><sup>-</sup>) to produce a deep blue charge-transfer complex. <b>Cellulose</b> consists of straight, rigid &beta;-sheets held tightly by inter-chain H-bonds with no helical cavities to accommodate iodine molecules.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q80: Write the complete sequence of events in Central Dogma of Molecular Biology.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>DNA &rarr; (Replication) &rarr; DNA &rarr; (Transcription) &rarr; mRNA &rarr; (Translation) &rarr; Functional Protein</b>.<br>Proposed by Francis Crick, representing the unidirectional flow of genetic information inside living cells.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.12); border-left: 5px solid #00B0FF; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 30px 0 20px 0; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">Section C: Long Answer (LA) Questions &amp; Derivations (5 Marks each)</h2>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q81: Comprehensive Chemical Proofs for the Open-Chain Structure of D-(+)-Glucose.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Unbranched 6-Carbon Chain:</b> Prolonged heating of glucose with <b>HI and Red Phosphorus at 373 K</b> yields <b>n-Hexane</b>: HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CHO + 14 HI &rarr; (Red P / 373 K) &rarr; <b>CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-CH<sub>3</sub></b> + 6 H<sub>2</sub>O + 7 I<sub>2</sub>.<br><br><b>2. Carbonyl Group (&gt;C=O):</b><br>&bull; Reacts with <b>Hydroxylamine (NH<sub>2</sub>OH)</b> to form <b>Glucose Oxime</b>: HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CHO + NH<sub>2</sub>OH &rarr; HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CH=N-OH + H<sub>2</sub>O.<br>&bull; Adds <b>HCN</b> to form <b>Glucose Cyanohydrin</b>: HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CHO + HCN &rarr; HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CH(OH)CN.<br><br><b>3. Carbonyl is an Aldehyde (-CHO):</b> Mild oxidation with <b>Bromine Water</b> converts glucose into <b>Gluconic acid</b>: HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CHO + [O] &rarr; (Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O) &rarr; <b>HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>COOH</b>.<br><br><b>4. Five Hydroxyl Groups (-OH):</b> Acetylation with <b>Acetic Anhydride</b> yields <b>Glucose Pentaacetate</b>: HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CHO + 5 (CH<sub>3</sub>CO)<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (ZnCl<sub>2</sub>) &rarr; <b>AcOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOAc)<sub>4</sub>CHO</b> + 5 CH<sub>3</sub>COOH.<br><br><b>5. One Primary Alcohol (-CH<sub>2</sub>OH):</b> Oxidation with concentrated <b>Nitric Acid (HNO<sub>3</sub>)</b> yields the dicarboxylic acid <b>Saccharic acid</b>: HOCH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>CHO + 3 [O] &rarr; (conc. HNO<sub>3</sub>) &rarr; <b>HOOC-(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>-COOH</b> + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>6. D-Configuration:</b> The -OH group on C-5 is on the right side in the Fischer projection.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q82: Cyclic Structure of D-Glucose: Why open-chain failed, Anomers (&alpha; vs &beta;), Haworth Pyranose rings, and Mutarotation.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Failure of Open-Chain Model:</b> (1) Does not restore Schiff's reagent colour; (2) Does not form NaHSO<sub>3</sub> bisulphite adduct; (3) Glucose pentaacetate does not form oxime; (4) Exists in two stable crystalline forms (&alpha; and &beta;).<br><br><b>2. Cyclic Hemiacetal Ring Formation:</b> Intramolecular nucleophilic addition of the C-5 hydroxyl group onto the C-1 aldehyde carbonyl produces a 6-membered <b>Glucopyranose</b> ring, generating a new stereocenter at <b>C-1 (Anomeric Carbon)</b>.<br><br><b>3. Anomers:</b><br>&bull; <b>&alpha;-D-(+)-Glucopyranose:</b> C-1 anomeric -OH points <b>downward</b> (trans to C-5 -CH<sub>2</sub>OH). Melting Point = 419 K, [&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = <b>+112&deg;</b>.<br>&bull; <b>&beta;-D-(+)-Glucopyranose:</b> C-1 anomeric -OH points <b>upward</b> (cis to C-5 -CH<sub>2</sub>OH). Melting Point = 423 K, [&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = <b>+19&deg;</b>.<br><br><b>4. Mutarotation Mechanism:</b> In water, the pyranose ring undergoes spontaneous reversible opening through the open-chain aldehyde form until dynamic equilibrium is established:<br><b>&alpha;-D-Glucose (+112&deg;, 36%) &#8652; Open Chain Form (0.02%) &#8652; &beta;-D-Glucose (+19&deg;, 64%)</b> &rarr; Resulting in a stable specific rotation of <b>+52.7&deg;</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q83: Comprehensive Structural Study of Disaccharides: Sucrose, Maltose, and Lactose with Glycosidic Linkages.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Sucrose (C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub>):</b><br>&bull; <b>Monomers:</b> &alpha;-D-Glucopyranose + &beta;-D-Fructofuranose.<br>&bull; <b>Linkage:</b> <b>&alpha;-1,&beta;-2 Glycosidic Linkage</b> between C-1 of glucose and C-2 of fructose.<br>&bull; <b>Nature:</b> Non-reducing sugar (no free hemiacetal/hemiketal OH); Invert sugar ([&alpha;]<sub>D</sub>: +66.5&deg; &rarr; -39.7&deg;).<br><br><b>2. Maltose (C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub>):</b><br>&bull; <b>Monomers:</b> Two &alpha;-D-Glucopyranose units.<br>&bull; <b>Linkage:</b> <b>&alpha;-1,4 Glycosidic Linkage</b> between C-1 of first glucose and C-4 of second glucose.<br>&bull; <b>Nature:</b> Reducing sugar (C-1 of second unit is free hemiacetal; undergoes mutarotation and reduces Fehling's).<br><br><b>3. Lactose (C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub>):</b><br>&bull; <b>Monomers:</b> &beta;-D-Galactopyranose + &beta;-D-Glucopyranose.<br>&bull; <b>Linkage:</b> <b>&beta;-1,4 Glycosidic Linkage</b> between C-1 of galactose and C-4 of glucose.<br>&bull; <b>Nature:</b> Reducing sugar (free hemiacetal -OH at C-1 of glucose).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q84: Polysaccharides Master Architecture: Starch (Amylose vs Amylopectin), Cellulose, and Glycogen.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Starch:</b> Main energy storage polysaccharide of plants.<br>&bull; <b>Amylose (15-20%):</b> Linear, unbranched chain of 200-1000 &alpha;-D-glucose units joined by <b>&alpha;-1,4-glycosidic bonds</b>. Water-soluble; forms helical coils that give deep blue colour with iodine.<br>&bull; <b>Amylopectin (80-85%):</b> Insoluble branched polymer. Linear chains contain <b>&alpha;-1,4-bonds</b>, with branch points occurring every 20-25 glucose units via <b>&alpha;-1,6-glycosidic bonds</b>.<br><br><b>2. Cellulose:</b> Structural component of plant cell walls.<br>&bull; Unbranched linear polymer of &beta;-D-glucose linked by <b>&beta;-1,4-glycosidic bonds</b>.<br>&bull; Extended chains align parallel to form hydrogen-bonded crystalline microfibrils of high tensile strength. Insoluble; cannot be digested by humans.<br><br><b>3. Glycogen:</b> Animal storage polysaccharide in liver and muscles.<br>&bull; Highly branched &alpha;-D-glucose polymer structurally similar to amylopectin but branching occurs every 8-12 glucose residues via &alpha;-1,6 bonds.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q85: Amino Acids: Classification, Zwitterion Chemistry, Isoelectric Point (pI), and Electrophoretic Mobility.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Structural Classification:</b><br>&bull; <b>Neutral (1 -NH<sub>2</sub>, 1 -COOH):</b> Glycine, Alanine, Valine.<br>&bull; <b>Acidic (1 -NH<sub>2</sub>, 2 -COOH):</b> Aspartic acid, Glutamic acid (pI &approx; 3).<br>&bull; <b>Basic (2 -NH<sub>2</sub>, 1 -COOH):</b> Lysine, Arginine, Histidine (pI &approx; 9-10).<br><br><b>2. Zwitterion Equilibrium:</b><br>In aqueous solution: H<sub>2</sub>N-CHR-COOH &#8652; <b>H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-CHR-COO<sup>-</sup> (Zwitterion)</b>.<br><br><b>3. Behaviour in Electric Field (Electrophoresis):</b><br>&bull; <b>At pH &lt; pI (Acidic):</b> Amino acid exists as Cation [H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-CHR-COOH] &rarr; migrates to <b>Cathode (-)</b>.<br>&bull; <b>At pH &gt; pI (Alkaline):</b> Amino acid exists as Anion [H<sub>2</sub>N-CHR-COO<sup>-</sup>] &rarr; migrates to <b>Anode (+)</b>.<br>&bull; <b>At pH = pI (Isoelectric Point):</b> Net charge is zero &rarr; <b>No migration</b> in electric field; minimum solubility.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q86: Four Structural Levels of Proteins: Primary, Secondary, Tertiary, and Quaternary Architecture.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Primary Structure (1&deg;):</b> The precise linear sequence of amino acids joined covalently by peptide bonds (-CO-NH-). Determines the higher order 3D folding.<br><br><b>2. Secondary Structure (2&deg;):</b> Conformation of polypeptide backbone held by <b>Hydrogen Bonds</b>:<br>&bull; <b>&alpha;-Helix:</b> Right-handed spiral stabilized by intramolecular H-bonds between C=O of residue n and N-H of residue n+4.<br>&bull; <b>&beta;-Pleated Sheet:</b> Polypeptide chains lie side by side held by intermolecular H-bonds in parallel/antiparallel sheets.<br><br><b>3. Tertiary Structure (3&deg;):</b> Complete 3D folding into compact <b>Globular</b> or extended <b>Fibrous</b> shapes, stabilized by:<br>&bull; Disulfide bridges (-S-S-), Salt bridges / ionic bonds (-NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup> &bull;&bull;&bull; <sup>-</sup>OOC-), Hydrogen bonds, and Hydrophobic interactions.<br><br><b>4. Quaternary Structure (4&deg;):</b> Assembly and spatial packing of multiple polypeptide subunits (e.g. <b>Haemoglobin</b> = 2&alpha; + 2&beta; chains + 4 Haem groups).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q87: Denaturation of Proteins: Molecular Mechanisms, Chemical Triggers, and Biological Consequences.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Definition:</b> Process where native proteins lose their tertiary, secondary, and quaternary structural folding upon exposure to physical or chemical stress, resulting in precipitation and permanent loss of biological activity.<br><br><b>2. Denaturing Triggers:</b><br>&bull; <b>Heat / Temperature:</b> Kinetic agitation ruptures delicate hydrogen bonds and hydrophobic interactions.<br>&bull; <b>pH Extremes:</b> Disrupts ionic salt bridges by altering ionization states of -NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup> and -COO<sup>-</sup> groups.<br>&bull; <b>Heavy Metal Salts (Ag<sup>+</sup>, Pb<sup>2+</sup>, Hg<sup>2+</sup>):</b> Precipitate proteins by binding to -SH groups.<br><br><b>3. Structural Fate:</b> Secondary, tertiary, and quaternary conformations unfold, but the <b>Primary Structure (covalent peptide bonds) remains 100% intact</b>.<br><br><b>4. Biological Consequences:</b> Complete loss of enzymatic, hormonal, or transport activity (e.g. Boiled egg white, curdling of milk).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q88: Watson-Crick Double Helix Model of DNA: Structural Parameters, Complementary Base Pairing, and 3D Dimensions.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Structural Architecture:</b><br>&bull; <b>Double Strands:</b> Two polynucleotide chains wound around a common central axis in a right-handed double helix.<br>&bull; <b>Antiparallel Orientation:</b> One strand runs 5&apos; &rarr; 3&apos; while the complementary strand runs 3&apos; &rarr; 5&apos;.<br>&bull; <b>Backbone &amp; Rungs:</b> Sugar-phosphate units form the external hydrophilic backbone; nitrogenous bases stack inside like rungs of a spiral staircase.<br><br><b>2. Complementary Base Pairing:</b><br>&bull; <b>Adenine pairs with Thymine:</b> A=T via <b>2 Hydrogen Bonds</b>.<br>&bull; <b>Guanine pairs with Cytosine:</b> G&equiv;C via <b>3 Hydrogen Bonds</b>.<br>&bull; Purine always pairs with pyrimidine, maintaining a uniform 2.0 nm helical diameter.<br><br><b>3. Helical Dimensions:</b><br>&bull; <b>Helix Diameter:</b> 2.0 nm (20 &Aring;).<br>&bull; <b>Pitch (one complete turn):</b> 3.4 nm (34 &Aring;).<br>&bull; <b>Base Pairs per Turn:</b> Exactly 10 base pairs per turn.<br>&bull; <b>Distance between adjacent Base Pairs:</b> 0.34 nm (3.4 &Aring;).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q89: Biological Functions of Nucleic Acids: Replication, Transcription, Translation, and DNA Fingerprinting.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Replication (Self-Duplication):</b> Semiconservative replication where each parental DNA strand serves as a template to synthesize an identical complementary daughter strand, ensuring faithful transmission of genetic traits.<br><br><b>2. Transcription (Information Transfer):</b> Synthesis of single-stranded mRNA from DNA template by RNA polymerase inside the nucleus.<br><br><b>3. Translation (Protein Biosynthesis):</b> mRNA moves to ribosomes where tRNA brings specific amino acids according to triplet codons to assemble polypeptide chains.<br><br><b>4. DNA Fingerprinting:</b> Identification of individuals by analyzing variable number tandem repeats (VNTRs) in genomic DNA. Unique for every individual (except identical twins); used in forensics, paternity verification, and evolutionary biology.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q90: Master Vitamin Encyclopedia: Fat-Soluble vs Water-Soluble, Chemical Names, Biological Functions, and Deficiency Syndromes.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Fat-Soluble Vitamins (Stored in Liver &amp; Adipose):</b><br>&bull; <b>Vitamin A (Retinol):</b> Visual pigment rhodopsin &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Night Blindness, Xerophthalmia.<br>&bull; <b>Vitamin D (Calciferol):</b> Ca<sup>2+</sup> absorption &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Rickets (children), Osteomalacia (adults).<br>&bull; <b>Vitamin E (Tocopherol):</b> Biological antioxidant &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Muscular dystrophy, Sterility.<br>&bull; <b>Vitamin K (Phylloquinone):</b> Prothrombin synthesis &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Haemorrhage, delayed blood clotting.<br><br><b>2. Water-Soluble Vitamins (Excreted in Urine, Daily Intake Needed):</b><br>&bull; <b>Vitamin B<sub>1</sub> (Thiamine):</b> Coenzyme TPP in carbohydrate metabolism &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Beriberi.<br>&bull; <b>Vitamin B<sub>2</sub> (Riboflavin):</b> FMN/FAD coenzymes &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Cheilosis, Glossitis.<br>&bull; <b>Vitamin B<sub>3</sub> (Niacin):</b> NAD<sup>+</sup>/NADP<sup>+</sup> &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Pellagra (4 Ds).<br>&bull; <b>Vitamin B<sub>6</sub> (Pyridoxine):</b> Amino acid transamination &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Convulsions.<br>&bull; <b>Vitamin B<sub>12</sub> (Cobalamin):</b> RBC maturation &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Pernicious Anaemia.<br>&bull; <b>Vitamin C (Ascorbic Acid):</b> Collagen hydroxylation &bull; <i>Deficiency:</i> Scurvy.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q91: Complete Chemical Analysis of Hormones: Classification, Mechanisms, and Physiological Homeostasis.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Definition:</b> Biochemical signaling molecules synthesized by ductless endocrine glands and released into the bloodstream to coordinate metabolic functions.<br><br><b>2. Chemical Classification:</b><br>&bull; <b>Steroid Hormones:</b> Lipid-soluble molecules with cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene core (<b>Testosterone, Progesterone, Estrogen, Cortisone</b>). Cross cell membranes to bind nuclear receptors.<br>&bull; <b>Peptide &amp; Protein Hormones:</b> Water-soluble (<b>Insulin</b> [51 amino acids], <b>Glucagon</b>). Bind cell-surface receptors and trigger secondary messengers (cAMP).<br>&bull; <b>Amino Acid Derivatives:</b> <b>Epinephrine / Adrenaline</b> (fight-or-flight), <b>Thyroxine</b> (iodinated tyrosine, regulates BMR).<br><br><b>3. Homeostatic Function:</b> Maintain blood glucose (Insulin lowers, Glucagon raises), regulate electrolyte balance, growth, and reproduction.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q92: Multi-Step Roadmap 1: Carbohydrate A (C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub>) is non-reducing. On hydrolysis with dilute acid, it gives an equimolar mixture of B and C (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub>). B on reaction with Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O gives gluconic acid. C gives a negative test with Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O. The specific rotation of A changes from positive to negative on hydrolysis. Identify A, B, C.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Sucrose (Cane Sugar, C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub>)</b> (non-reducing disaccharide, undergoes inversion).<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>D-(+)-Glucose</b> (aldohexose, oxidized by Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O to gluconic acid).<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>D-(-)-Fructose</b> (ketohexose, resists Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O oxidation).<br><b>Reaction:</b> C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub> (+66.5&deg;) + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; D-(+)-Glucose (+52.7&deg;) + D-(-)-Fructose (-92.4&deg;) &rarr; Net [&alpha;]<sub>D</sub> = -39.7&deg; (Invert Sugar).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q93: Multi-Step Roadmap 2: A tripeptide on complete hydrolysis yields 2 moles of Glycine and 1 mole of Alanine. The N-terminal amino acid is Alanine. Write the structure, complete IUPAC name, and chemical formula of the tripeptide.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Deduction:</b><br>1. The N-terminal is <b>Alanine (H<sub>2</sub>N-CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)-COOH)</b>.<br>2. The remaining two residues are <b>Glycine (H<sub>2</sub>N-CH<sub>2</sub>-COOH)</b>.<br>3. Sequence: <b>Alanyl-glycyl-glycine (Ala-Gly-Gly)</b>.<br><br><b>Structure:</b> <b>H<sub>2</sub>N-CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)-CO-NH-CH<sub>2</sub>-CO-NH-CH<sub>2</sub>-COOH</b>.<br><b>Formula:</b> C<sub>7</sub>H<sub>13</sub>N<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub> (Contains 2 peptide bonds).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q94: Multi-Step Roadmap 3: An aldohexose A (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub>) on reaction with HI/P gives n-hexane. A on reaction with Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O gives B. A on reaction with conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> gives C. B on reaction with conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> also gives C. Identify A, B, C.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>D-Glucose [HOCH<sub>2</sub>-(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>-CHO]</b>.<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Gluconic acid [HOCH<sub>2</sub>-(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>-COOH]</b> (oxidation of -CHO group).<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>Saccharic acid [HOOC-(CHOH)<sub>4</sub>-COOH]</b> (dicarboxylic acid from oxidation of both terminal groups).<br><b>Equations:</b><br>&bull; Glucose + Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; Gluconic acid (B).<br>&bull; Glucose + conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> &rarr; Saccharic acid (C).<br>&bull; Gluconic acid + conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> &rarr; Saccharic acid (C).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q95: Structure and Synthesis of Insulin: Two-chain architecture, Disulfide bridges, and Artificial synthesis.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Primary Sequence &amp; Mass:</b> Insulin is a globular protein hormone composed of <b>51 amino acid residues</b> with molecular mass 5734 u.<br><br><b>2. Two Polypeptide Chains:</b><br>&bull; <b>Chain A:</b> Contains <b>21 amino acids</b>.<br>&bull; <b>Chain B:</b> Contains <b>30 amino acids</b>.<br><br><b>3. Disulfide Cross-Links:</b><br>&bull; <b>Two Inter-chain Disulfide Bonds:</b> Connect Chain A (Cys-7 and Cys-20) to Chain B (Cys-7 and Cys-19).<br>&bull; <b>One Intra-chain Disulfide Bond:</b> Formed within Chain A between Cys-6 and Cys-11.<br><br><b>4. Biological Function:</b> Secreted by &beta;-cells of islets of Langerhans in pancreas; facilitates cellular uptake and utilization of glucose. First protein whose complete primary sequence was determined (by Frederick Sanger in 1953).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q96: Mechanism of Action and Classification of Enzymes (IUBMB System).\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. International Classification (6 Major Classes - 'OTH LIL'):</b><br>1. <b>Oxidoreductases:</b> Catalyze oxidation-reduction reactions (e.g. Alcohol dehydrogenase).<br>2. <b>Transferases:</b> Transfer functional groups between substrates (e.g. Hexokinase).<br>3. <b>Hydrolases:</b> Catalyze hydrolytic cleavage of bonds (e.g. Invertase, Lipase).<br>4. <b>Lyases:</b> Cleave bonds by elimination leaving double bonds without hydrolysis (e.g. Aldolase).<br>5. <b>Isomerases:</b> Catalyze geometric or structural isomerizations (e.g. Glucose isomerase).<br>6. <b>Ligases / Synthetases:</b> Join two molecules coupled with ATP hydrolysis (e.g. DNA ligase).<br><br><b>2. Catalytic Mechanism:</b> Accelerate rates by stabilizing the transition state, optimizing catalytic residue orientations, and drastically lowering Gibbs free energy of activation (&Delta;G<sup>&Dagger;</sup>).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q97: Structural Details of Disaccharides: Haworth Projections and Ring Carbon Numbering of Maltose and Lactose.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Maltose (4-O-(&alpha;-D-Glucopyranosyl)-&alpha;-D-glucopyranose):</b><br>&bull; Non-reducing unit: &alpha;-D-Glucopyranose (C-1 involved in glycosidic link).<br>&bull; Reducing unit: &alpha;-D-Glucopyranose (C-4 involved in glycosidic link, C-1 has free hemiacetal -OH).<br>&bull; Joined by an <b>&alpha;-1,4-glycosidic bond</b>.<br><br><b>2. Lactose (4-O-(&beta;-D-Galactopyranosyl)-&beta;-D-glucopyranose):</b><br>&bull; Non-reducing unit: &beta;-D-Galactopyranose (differs from glucose at C-4 epimeric center where -OH is upward).<br>&bull; Reducing unit: &beta;-D-Glucopyranose (free hemiacetal -OH at C-1).<br>&bull; Joined by a <b>&beta;-1,4-glycosidic bond</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q98: Quantitative Comparison between Nucleic Acids and Proteins: Macromolecular Architecture and Central Dogma Roles.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Monomeric Units &amp; Linkages:</b><br>&bull; Proteins: &alpha;-Amino acids joined by covalent <b>Peptide linkages (-CO-NH-)</b>.<br>&bull; Nucleic Acids: Nucleotides joined by <b>3&apos;-5&apos; Phosphodiester linkages</b>.<br><br><b>2. Higher-Order Structural Conformations:</b><br>&bull; Proteins: 1&deg; (sequence) &rarr; 2&deg; (&alpha;-helix/&beta;-sheet) &rarr; 3&deg; (globular/fibrous) &rarr; 4&deg; (subunits).<br>&bull; Nucleic Acids: Primary nucleotide sequence &rarr; Secondary double helix (DNA) or hairpin loops (tRNA).<br><br><b>3. Genetic Information Flow:</b> Nucleic acids store and transmit the blueprint, while proteins execute cellular catalysts, structural scaffolding, and physiological regulation.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q99: Comprehensive Diagnostic Master Scheme for Biomolecules: Carbohydrates, Proteins, Lipids, and Vitamins.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Carbohydrate Tests:</b><br>&bull; <b>Molisch's Test:</b> &alpha;-Naphthol + conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; <b>Purple / Violet ring</b> at interface (all carbohydrates).<br>&bull; <b>Fehling's / Benedict's Test:</b> Reducing sugars form <b>red Cu<sub>2</sub>O precipitate</b>.<br>&bull; <b>Iodine Test:</b> Starch gives <b>deep blue colour</b>; glycogen gives reddish-brown.<br><br><b>2. Protein Tests:</b><br>&bull; <b>Biuret Test:</b> Alkaline CuSO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; <b>Violet / Purple colour</b> (detects &ge;2 peptide bonds).<br>&bull; <b>Ninhydrin Test:</b> Ninhydrin reagent &rarr; <b>Ruhemann's purple / Blue colour</b> (detects free &alpha;-amino acids).<br>&bull; <b>Xanthoproteic Test:</b> Conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> &rarr; <b>Yellow colour</b> (turns orange with NaOH; detects aromatic amino acids).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 255, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00B0FF; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q100: Biomolecules Master Summary &amp; Interconnections.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Carbohydrates:</b> Monosaccharides (Glucose, Fructose) &rarr; Disaccharides (Sucrose, Maltose, Lactose) &rarr; Polysaccharides (Starch, Cellulose, Glycogen). Main energy suppliers and structural cell walls.<br><br><b>2. Proteins:</b> &alpha;-Amino acids (Zwitterions) &rarr; Peptides &rarr; 1&deg;, 2&deg;, 3&deg;, 4&deg; Protein Structures. Structural building blocks, biocatalytic enzymes, and regulatory hormones.<br><br><b>3. Nucleic Acids:</b> Nucleotides &rarr; DNA (Heredity, double helix, A=T, G&equiv;C) and RNA (Protein synthesis).<br><br><b>4. Vitamins &amp; Lipids:</b> Essential micronutrients (Fat-soluble A, D, E, K; Water-soluble B-complex, C) and metabolic energy storage lipids.</div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n";

